From: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:39:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] hvf: arm: complete unknown SMCs with NOT_SUPPORTED instead of
 UNDEF

With accel=hvf the virt machine uses HVC as its PSCI conduit, since
there is no EL3. An SMC from the guest was then answered with an UNDEF.
KVM treats the same SMC as an SMCCC call and returns
SMCCC_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED, which is also what firmware without the
service does.

Windows 11 23H2 (22631) relies on that: its winload issues SMC
0xc3000001 early in boot and has no handler for the UNDEF, so the
exception lands on a `b .` vector and the guest hangs at "Start boot
option" with one vCPU spinning. Seen on a guest created by Parallels
Desktop and moved to QEMU. With this change it boots to the desktop.

Step over the SMC and return -1 in x0, as the SMC-conduit path already
does for calls PSCI does not handle.
---
 target/arm/hvf/hvf.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c b/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c
index 8d6767c..7bea5c1 100644
--- a/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c
+++ b/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c
@@ -2580,8 +2580,15 @@ static int hvf_handle_exception(CPUState *cpu, hv_vcpu_exit_exception_t *excp)
                 env->xregs[0] = -1;
             }
         } else {
+            /*
+             * There is no EL3 behind an HVF guest, but guests still probe
+             * SMCCC services with SMC (Windows 11 23H2's winload issues
+             * 0xc3000001 and has no handler for the UNDEF). Do what KVM
+             * does: complete the call with SMCCC_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED.
+             */
+            advance_pc = true;
             trace_hvf_unknown_smc(env->xregs[0]);
-            hvf_raise_exception(cpu, EXCP_UDEF, syn_uncategorized(), 1);
+            env->xregs[0] = -1;
         }
         break;
     case EC_INSNABORT: {
